Wow! I had no idea (and still have a hard time believing) that=20
"Spacewar!" was commercially published, but if you say so I'll certainly=20
try to believe you! (Though I've read that in 1971 a slightly updated=20
"Spacewar!" called "Galaxy Game", running on a later PDP model, was put=20
in a university coffee shop as perhaps the first coin-op.)

IMO you are perfectly correct in saying that mainframe based games=20
existed before "Spacewar!" (and also discrete circuitry ones), but I=20
think it's a (the?) game most commonly referred to as "the first video=20
game", I would be surprised to see something else given this title in=20
such a context (except perhaps PONG, definitely in error).

Perhaps "Spacewar!" could be called "the first video digital computer=20
game", to be precise? There certainly were earlier computer games, but=20
probably only non-video (video as in *moving* images, ie not counting=20
the OXO game), and earlier graphical 'video' games, but probably only=20
non-digital-computer ones.

Now I just found this very nice page at Wikipedia, trying to bring some=20
clarity to the (apparently) debated issue:
http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_video_game

Following that (and following my made-up rule set from above):
1947 - 'missile simulator' - non-digital
1951 - NIM - non-video (just four rows of bulbs to indicate numbers)
1952 - OXO - non-video (static images - but so very close!)
1958 - Tennis for Two - non-digital
1961[sic] - Spacewar! - !

Perhaps, in light of the origins of Nethack<-Hack<-Rogue<-Colossal Cave,=20
we should rather be looking for "the first mainframe computer based=20
video game"? I guess "Spacewar!" could be up for this title? But then=20
again, if we are using these origins as a base, why should we discount=20
early non-video mainframe games which I would think there could be some=20
(the PDP-1 had two years before "Spacewars!" for example)...

Well, since the text author even confused "Spacewars!" with "Star Wars"=20
I guess we can't expect too much really. :)

